// PARKED FINDING (2026-07-05) -- ARRAY/aggregate field DEFAULTS are broken two ways,
// both SILENT, and the fix is a design/backend call (not a unilateral surface change):
//
// (1) SILENTLY DROPPED. A VALID array default is not emitted: `xs: [i32;3] = [1,2,3]`
//     then reading `xs[2]` returns 0, not 3 (scalar defaults ARE emitted -- `x: i32 = 42`
//     reads back 42 -- so emission is scalar-only; arrays/carriers/aggregates are ignored).
//     Per "no silent anything" this declared default silently does nothing.
// (2) UNVALIDATED. Length + element class are unchecked at this position: `[i32;2] =
//     [1,2,3,4]` (too many) and `[i32;3] = [true,2,3]` (cross-class element) both compile.
//     `validate_array_literal_elements` (struct_literals.rs) already does length + element
//     class + narrowing, but it takes `&Machine`/`&State` and is NOT called from data.rs's
//     field loop (which only got the machine-free SCALAR checks 2026-07-05).
//
// DECISION NEEDED (Zach): either (a) SUPPORT array field defaults (emit them, then wire
// validate_array_literal_elements into data.rs via the Option<&Machine> generalization the
// scalar fix already started), or (b) REJECT non-scalar field defaults with a clear
// diagnostic ("aggregate field defaults are not emitted; initialize in a machine body")
// so the silent drop becomes a visible error. Do NOT half-validate a silently-dropped value.
// Not a live memory-corruption bug (defaults not emitted = no overflow); a hygiene/semantics
// gap. Repro below is the too-many-elements form (would overflow IF array defaults emitted).

boundary trait Console { machine exit_process(return_code: i32); }
data Main { console: Console; xs: [i32; 2] = [1, 2, 3, 4]; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    let g: i32 = self.xs[0];
    self.console.exit_process(g);
}
